# Proposal

## The problem (problem)
For each of this prototype's own checks, name a rewrite of an input under which the check's verdict ought to move. Propose a kernel, a transform, and an input. The executor will run it; the catalogue says which pairs anyone has written down already. A pair that moves and is not catalogued is a boundary nobody has recorded; a pair the catalogue says moves and that does not move is a defect in the catalogue.

## Proposals so far (earlier)
[ac1042606b813c15] (mini.proposal.v1)
A proposal here is a triple: a kernel (one of the prototype's own checks), a transform (a rewrite), and an input stated as it stands before the rewrite. The executor applies the transform and re-runs the kernel; the pair moves iff the verdict differs before and after. Whether a moving pair is already recorded in the catalogue, and whether any catalogue-claimed motion fails to reproduce, are judgements I cannot make for the reason below, so I state expected motions and leave classification to the run.

Limitation, stated up front. The one catalogue block I can see (c82a3c8063d2d61a) arrives truncated: I can read its identity and the first words of its note, but not the pair list or the move flags. Two consequences. First, I claim novelty for nothing — any of my pairs may already be written down. Second, I cannot nominate a defect case (a pair the catalogue says moves that does not move), because I cannot read what the catalogue says moves; covering that direction needs the full catalogue text first.

Since the prototype's own check list is likewise not visible to me, I aim at the two check shapes a mini kernel-boundaries prototype most plausibly contains: an exact-match checker and a structural parser checker. If the real checks differ, these triples should be re-aimed rather than run as-is.

Triple 1. Kernel: equals-'hello' — accept iff the input is exactly the characters h,e,l,l,o. Transform: trim — strip leading and trailing whitespace. Input: ' hello ' (space-prefixed and space-suffixed). Expected motion: reject → accept. The kernel is byte-strict; the transform removes the only difference. If the run shows no motion, either the transform is not trimming or the kernel is not exact-match, and the run should say which.

Triple 2. Kernel: equals-'hello' (same kernel). Transform: lowercase (ASCII case folding). Input: 'HELLO'. Expected motion: reject → accept. Same kernel as Triple 1 with an independent rewrite: if both move, the kernel's boundary is sensitive to two separate rewrites; if only one moves, the run itself identifies which rewrite the kernel tolerates.

Triple 3. Kernel: json-number — accept iff the whole input parses as a JSON number. Transform: prepend '-'. Input: '1'. Expected motion: accept → reject, since '--1' is not a JSON number. This probes the direction the first two triples do not: a rewrite that breaks an accepting input rather than repairing a rejecting one.

Triple 4. Kernel: json-number (same kernel). Transform: append 'e'. Input: '1'. Expected motion: accept → reject ('1e' has an exponent marker with no exponent). Same kernel and same input as Triple 3 with a different transform, both expected to move the same way — a cheap consistency check; if one moves and the other does not, the difference lies in the transform implementations, not the kernel.

Not claimed here: that any of these pairs is absent from the catalogue; that any catalogue entry is defective. Priority if only one triple is run: Triple 1 — the smallest complete demonstration of kernel, transform, input, and a stated expected motion the run can confirm or refute.
