[e3ad7a1bde02d5d2] (mini.proposal.v1)
This proposal fills the one cell the earlier proposals leave open and repairs one mis-specified expectation they leave behind, so that after this run each kernel the earlier proposals aimed at is crossed in both directions, with no expectation left that the run must refute for reasons outside the kernel.

Triple 6. Kernel: equals-'hello' — accept iff the input is exactly the characters h,e,l,l,o (the same kernel as Triples 1 and 2 of ac1042606b813c15). Transform: append 'x'. Input: 'hello'. Expected motion: accept → reject.

Why the motion is expected. The kernel is byte-strict, and 'hellox' is the accepted string with one character added. Appending is the minimal breaking rewrite: it alters nothing already correct and introduces exactly one fault, so any whole-input comparison against the literal rejects it.

What the run distinguishes. This is the triple that separates exact-match from its laxer neighbours, which Triples 1 and 2 leave indistinguishable: trim on ' hello ' and lowercase on 'HELLO' move under a starts-with reading exactly as they do under an exact reading, so a starts-with kernel looks identical to an exact-match kernel in the record so far. (A contains reading is already disfavoured by Triple 1, since ' hello ' contains 'hello' and so would not move under it.) 'hellox' is accepted by both a starts-with-'hello' check and a contains-'hello' check, so this triple moves only under exact-match: motion confirms the byte-strict reading, and stillness refutes it in favour of one of the laxer readings. If it is still, the probe that separates the two survivors is a prepend — 'xhello' is contains-yes but starts-with-no — which I name here but do not formally propose.

Composition. With this triple the exact-match kernel carries two repair-direction rewrites (trim, lowercase) and one breaking rewrite, mirroring the json-number kernel's two breaking rewrites and one repair. The accepting set of this kernel is a single point, so every rewrite direction leaves it; the record should show crossings on both kernels in both directions.

Triple 7 — a correction, not a new cell. Triple 3 of ac1042606b813c15 is mis-specified as written: prepending '-' to '1' yields '-1', which is a valid JSON number under JSON.parse and under the strict grammar alike, so that pair should stand still, not move. Its stated justification — '--1' is not a JSON number — implies the intended input was '-1'. I propose the intended pair so the record gains the crossing instead of an unexplained non-motion: kernel json-number, transform prepend '-', input '-1', expected motion accept → reject ('--1' carries two minus signs and parses under no JSON number reading). Triple 4 already crosses the breaking direction on this kernel, so this adds no cell; it adds a second, independent breaking rewrite, matching the pattern of Triples 1 and 2, and it replaces an expectation whose failure would falsely implicate the transform or the kernel with one whose stillness would mean only that the original input was typed wrong: '-1' is simply a number.

Standing limitations, unchanged from the earlier proposals. The one catalogue block visible to me (c82a3c8063d2d61a) is truncated to the first words of its note; the pair list and move flags lie beyond the cut, so I claim novelty for nothing and nominate no defect case — classification is left to the run against the full catalogue text. The prototype's own check list is likewise not visible; both triples assume the two kernels the earlier proposals aimed at and should be re-aimed if the real checks differ.

Remaining cells after this run: none for the two guessed kernels — each then has repair- and breaking-direction coverage. What stays open is whether the prototype contains checks beyond these two shapes; only the run can show that.

Priority if only one triple is run: Triple 6 — the sole probe that separates exact-match from the starts-with reading, completing the cell the previous proposal named and left open.
